<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute low back strain (S39.012A)</content>. Mechanical low back pain without red flags or nerve-root signs. No imaging or blood tests are needed. Most such pain improves within weeks; heat and staying active help most. Among medicines, an NSAID is the first choice, but with sertraline it raises the risk of stomach bleeding, so a short course is taken with food and a proton pump inhibitor. Tramadol is avoided because with sertraline it can cause serotonin toxicity and seizures, and cyclobenzaprine because it is serotonergic and sedating; acetaminophen adds little for back pain. [S]<list><item>Heat to the back several times a day; keep walking; avoid bed rest.</item><item>Ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily with food for five days, with omeprazole 20 mg daily for the same five days.</item><item>Do not take tramadol, other opioids, or muscle relaxants with sertraline unless a clinician advises it.</item><item>Return at once for leg weakness, numbness in the groin, trouble passing urine, or fever.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder on sertraline (F33.2)</content>. Mood steady. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 100 mg daily.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tourette syndrome (F95.2)</content>. Tics worse with pain and poor sleep, as expected; they should settle as the pain does. [S]<list><item>Continue the competing-response practice from behavioral therapy.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (Qaseem A, et al. Ann Intern Med 2017;166(7):514-530) (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Phone check in one week. Six-month review in four weeks. Next scheduled visit 1765-09-28: six-month review of depression and tics. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
